==> kind create cluster --name promptforge-ai
Creates a new local Kubernetes cluster running inside a Docker container (that's what kind = "Kubernetes IN Docker" does).
--name promptforge-ai names it so you can run multiple separate clusters on the same machine and distinguish them.
This also automatically updates your kubeconfig and switches your current kubectl context to the new cluster.

==> kind get clusters
Lists all kind clusters currently running on your machine (just the names, one per line).
Useful for checking whether a cluster exists at all before troubleshooting kubectl connection errors —
if the name you expect isn't in this list, that cluster doesn't exist (which is exactly what we hit earlier when your cluster had been deleted).

==> kubectl get services -n promptforge-apps
Lists all Kubernetes Service objects in the promptforge-apps namespace — shows name, type (ClusterIP/ExternalName/etc.),
internal cluster IP, external IP (if any), and exposed ports. -n <namespace> scopes the query to that one namespace instead of just default.

==> kubectl get pods -n promptforge-apps
Lists all running Pods in that namespace — shows name, READY (containers ready / total containers, e.g. 2/2),
STATUS (Running, Pending, ImagePullBackOff, etc.), RESTARTS, and AGE.
This is usually the first command you run to check overall health of a namespace.

==> kubectl describe pod redis-server-7b66695cf-rfvtk -n promptforge-apps
Shows full details of one specific pod by name: its labels, containers, images, resource requests/limits, volumes, node
it's scheduled on, current status/conditions, and — critically —
the Events section at the bottom, which is the event log (scheduling, image pulls, container starts, crashes, errors) that tells you why a pod is failing.
This is the command you reach for when get pods shows something unhealthy and you need to know the root cause.